  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n\n\nThrough Sunday...\n\nGOES East water vapor and visible satellite indicate the cold \ncore upper low is now centered just east of Lake Erie with a \ntrough axis down into the Appalachians. A second upper low resides\nover the Baja California into Desert Southwest. Cooler and drier \nair will continue to dominate this pattern locally in the wake of \na recent dry, reinforcing cold front. Increased isentropic ascent \nand cirrus now reside over much of the area with mid to high level\ncloudiness persisting through the weekend. 1025 mb surface high \nwill build in late weekend into the Mid South, bringing much \ncooler temperatures through tomorrow (low to mid 70s F highs for \nSunday). Seasonable lows are expected this weekend with lows 33 F \nto 43 F tonight and Sunday night. Patchy frost is possible this \nweekend as winds become calm Sunday night and effective \nradiational cooling becomes more likely. The dry soil conditions \nwill support overachieving of the nocturnal cooling processes. At \nthe same time, there remains potential for cirrus cloud decks to \nstubbornly stick around this weekend downstream of the SW US upper\nlow, which could keep temperatures from dropping as much as they \nwould in clear sky conditions. Putting these factors together \nyields uncertainty for low temperatures and potential frost \nimpacts. Regardless, frost potential remains a concern, especially\nin low-lying and sheltered areas. Ensure plants are covered or \nindoors and consideration for pets and people cold temperatures \nwith frost to light freeze potential persists. Kept inherited \nLimited HWO graphic with minimal adjustments for patchy frost \nduring these nights, mainly in north-central, east to northeast \nMS.\n\nThrough next week...\n\nQuiet, cloudy and mostly dry pattern will persist into next week.\nMoist, isentropic ascent and cloudy conditions will remain draped\nacross the Gulf Coast states through at least mid week \n(Wednesday). Isolated rain shower is possible around midweek, but \nprobs remain isolated and low (less than 15 percent). Upper low is\nstill projected to become open shortwave, ascent will be less \nprominent as it propagates eastward. With some thermal temps \nwarming, highs will moderate to seasonably through mid week (69 F \nto 77 F Monday to 73 F to 79 F by Wednesday). Lows will remain \nseasonable. The synoptic pattern will feature a brief shortwave \nridge passage early week before the eastern half of the country \nbecomes dominated by deep layer troughing. With limited airmass \nrecovery in the boundary layer likely under this suppressed \nregime, a drier and cooler pattern into late week continues to \nlook likely. This could include another round of patchy frost or \nlight freeze in the day 6-8 range. Winds generally will be light \nthrough much of the period but clear days with full sun and \nassociated deep mixing with little rain in sight could result in \nincreased wildfire danger by Friday. /DC/EC/86/",
